# Feature Audit: Runtime Sessions and Durable Execution

## 1. Scope

This audit covers runtime sessions, runtime actions, durable execution claims, session binding, checkpoints, memory/session separation, isolation metadata, runtime observability, and SDK support. It inspects `product_platform/runtime`, runtime migrations, runtime routes in `api/app.py`, `agent-hypervisor` checkpoint code, `agent-runtime`, frontend runtime pages, and the Python SDK. Relevant benchmarks are AWS Bedrock AgentCore Runtime, LangGraph threads/runs/checkpoints, LangSmith runs, Google session/memory separation, and Temporal durable execution semantics. Out of scope: saga-specific compensation detail, covered in SAG.

## 2. Current Implementation Summary

Runtime records exist in `packages/product-platform/src/product_platform/db/migrations/0018_runtime_sessions.up.sql`, with `runtime_sessions` and `runtime_actions`. Models live in `packages/product-platform/src/product_platform/runtime/models.py`; repository logic in `runtime/repository.py`; sandbox validation in `runtime/sandbox.py`; saga execution in `runtime/saga_executor.py`. Runtime API routes exist around `api/app.py:8902` and saga execution routes around `api/app.py:9278`. UI calls are in `frontend/src/api/runtime.ts`, with runtime screens in `frontend/src/features/runtime/RuntimePage.tsx`.

The runtime currently persists session/action status, metadata, latency, and correlation IDs. It validates some agent state before creating sessions. Durable execution is represented mainly by saga builder/executor records and demo-safe action execution, not by replayable event history or persisted step checkpoints. The Python SDK focuses on Tool Gateway calls and has no first-class session/run/checkpoint API.

